  "body": "Process change: yes\n\nFor the maintainer's decision - a process change (xtask). Measured before/after on the same Hetzner ccx43 (16 cores): numbers follow in a comment.\n\n## Why\n\nOn a 16-core server the release gate used every core for the ~10 min of builds, then ~80% of the run used one or two cores. Three causes in `xtask/src`: `lanes::alone()` sent every camcraft-app / camcraft-kernel step to run alone, one after another; `lanes::waves` grouped the other steps into barrier waves that each waited for their slowest lane; and the alone steps used the default budget of 6.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **A. A narrower alone list** (`lanes.rs`). Builds (`--no-run`), clippy, every UI step (`in_ui` or camcraft-ui) and an app/kernel step that is not one named target still run alone. Of the single app/kernel targets, only `lanes::ISOLATED` runs alone. Every other app/kernel target now joins the shared lanes.\n- **B. A work queue in place of barrier waves** (new `queue.rs`). The builds run first, each alone. Then `budget::lane_limit` lanes of `budget::per_lane` jobs each pull the next step as they finish. The alone steps run last, one at a time, with the whole budget. The queue runs the longest steps first (Graham's LPT rule), using the per-step seconds the previous release run wrote to `gate-logs/release-*/timings.tsv`. Every release run now writes that file. A step with no recorded time ranks with the longest, and with no history at all the queue keeps plan order.\n- The checkout guard (VOID) now runs before every queued step. A failed build still stops the run, and a failed suite still stops nothing.\n- **C. Alone steps use the whole budget for `--test-threads`.** This already happens through `budget::scale` (the plan's `6` is the default budget). The gate now prints a note when the machine has more cores than the budget, because `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS=16` is what lets a 16-core runner use all its cores. **cut.yml should set it.**\n- `--dry-run` still prints the whole plan, with the queue in the order the lanes pull it. On this tree that is 2 builds, 1019 queued steps and ~60 alone steps.\n\n## The named alone list (`lanes::ISOLATED`) and why\n\nThese are the targets whose tests assert on wall-clock time, so a busy neighbour could turn them red. They were found by grepping the suites for `recv_timeout`, `deadline`, `Instant`/`elapsed` and `Duration::from_secs`. Suites that only print a timing are not listed.\n\n- `app:lib` and `kernel:lib`: watchdogs in the libs' own tests (`step_guard_tests`, `step_division_tests`, `solidcore_step`)\n- `app:import_step_closed_shell`, `import_step_corner_blend`, `import_step_curve_guard`, `import_step_guard`, `import_step_guard_default_cap`, `import_step_open_shell`: STEP import watchdogs (`within(limit, ..)` panics on a timeout)\n- `app:large_models` (streaming import < 60 s), `mcp_live` (TCP deadlines of 0.3-20 s), `shoulder_adaptive` (< 60 s), `step_nesting` (refusal < 1 s), `stock_field_cost` (per-program budget), `stock_play`, `stock_play_partial`, `stock_play_precalc` (worker deadlines)\n- `kernel:heal_import`, `import_corpus`, `mesh_deviation_bench`, `solidcraft_hostile`, `solidcraft_import`, `step_agreement`, `step_import`: built on the `step_util` / `smb_util` watchdogs\n\nA new suite with a wall-clock assertion has to be added to this list. A test pins that every listed binary exists.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `cargo test -p xtask`: all pass (161 unit tests plus the integration binaries). These tests were rewritten for the queue: lanes (schedule split, the ISOLATED entries exist, target keys, longest-first order, history picks the newest release `timings.tsv`), the executor fixture (overlap, failure, build stop, VOID), and budget (the per-lane `-j` and test-thread sums stay within the budget, and alone steps get the full budget).\n- `cargo clippy -p xtask --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- Not exercised: a full release gate run. That is the measurement in the comment to follow.\n\n## Risks\n\n- More app/kernel binaries now run beside each other, so peak memory is higher (the app heavy release lanes especially). The suites already use per-process temp dirs (`std::process::id()`), and that was spot-checked.\n- A timing-sensitive test missed by the grep could flake under load. Adding it to `ISOLATED` fixes that.\n\n## Revert\n\nRevert this commit. The plan steps themselves are unchanged, so nothing else depends on it. To get back only the old alone rule, make `lanes::alone` return true for any `camcraft-app` / `camcraft-kernel` argument.\n\n`PROCESS_VERSION` is not bumped here; the merging session bumps it.\n\nChangelog: none\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n\nFixes #2466\n",
